\section{Experiments}\label{sec:experiments}

\subsection{Experimental Setup}\label{sec:setup}

\paragraph{Datasets.}
We evaluate on four English NER benchmarks spanning a difficulty gradient.
\textbf{SciERC} \cite{luan2018scierc} (551 test instances, 6 entity types) is the primary medium-difficulty test bed (${\approx}$65\% F1).
\textbf{Few-NERD} \cite{ding2021fewnerd} (INTRA coarse; 37{,}648 instances, 8 entity types, ${\approx}$75\% F1) provides a low-degeneracy contrast where LP selection succeeds (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).
\textbf{CoNLL-2003} \cite{tjongkimsang2003conll} (3{,}453 test sentences, 4 entity types, ${\approx}$91\% F1) serves as a ceiling-effect control.
\textbf{WNUT-17} \cite{derczynski2017wnut} (1{,}287 social media instances, 51\% zero F1) probes the signal-collapse boundary.

\paragraph{Models and sampling.}
\textbf{Qwen3-8B} and \textbf{LLaMA-3.1-8B-Instruct} are LoRA fine-tuned using LLaMA Factory \cite{zheng2024llamafactory} (Appendix~\ref{app:training}; Few-NERD uses 3 epochs vs.\ 5 for others; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).
$N{=}8$ samples at $T{=}1.0$ (primary) with vLLM constrained decoding \cite{willard2023guided}; $N{=}16$ for scaling (Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Quality Estimation Results}\label{sec:qe_results}

\begin{table*}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{NER quality estimation ($\rho$ and AUROC; $N{=}8$, primary seed${=}42$; 3-seed $\sigma{\leq}0.026$ in Table~\ref{tab:multiseed}). AUROC binarizes at per-dataset median F1. Best in \textbf{bold}. RE in Appendix~\ref{app:re_extension}; $N{=}16$ in Table~\ref{tab:robustness}.}
\label{tab:quality_estimation}
\begin{tabular}{ll cc cc cc}
\toprule
& & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{SciERC}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (Qwen)}} & \multicolumn{2}{c}{\textbf{CoNLL (LLaMA)}} \\
\cmidrule(lr){3-4} \cmidrule(lr){5-6} \cmidrule(lr){7-8}
Signal & Family & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC & $\rho$ & AUC \\
\midrule
VC & Structural & \textbf{.379} & .684 & .424 & .789 & .426 & .709 \\
SJ  & Structural & .360 & .692 & .436 & .801 & \textbf{.431} & .704 \\
FK & Surface & .267 & .629 & .400 & .774 & .428 & .703 \\
EM  & Surface    & .295 & \textbf{.749} & \textbf{.451} & \textbf{.816} & \textbf{.431} & .705 \\
\midrule
LP     & Model      & .205 & .578 & .316 & .753 & .307 & \textbf{.761} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table*}

Table~\ref{tab:quality_estimation} presents $\rho$ and AUROC for five signals across tasks and models.

\paragraph{Signal rankings are difficulty-dependent.}
On SciERC, structural signals lead (VC $\rho{=}0.379$, SJ $0.360$) over LP ($0.205$); bootstrap CIs confirm the gap ($\Delta\rho{=}{+}0.155$; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_signal}).
On CoNLL (F1${\approx}$0.91), EM leads both $\rho$ and AUROC ($0.816$).
At the signal-collapse boundary (WNUT-17, F1${\approx}$42\%), full-set correlations are dominated by the zero/non-zero boundary (VC $\rho{=}0.724$ at $N{=}16$, but conditional $\rho{=}0.038$; Table~\ref{tab:wnut}); LP shows the reverse (conditional $\rho{=}0.350$; Figure~\ref{fig:signal_heatmap}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Cross-Dataset Contrast: Few-NERD}\label{sec:fewnerd}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{LP selection across datasets (Qwen3-8B, $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, gold-filtered). Few-NERD/CoNLL: 3-seed means; SciERC: single-seed. $p$: paired bootstrap.}
\label{tab:fewnerd}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccccr@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & $n$ & Greedy & Degen.\% & LP $\rho$ & LP $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
WNUT-17 & 1{,}287 & .452 & 67.9\% & $-$.446 & $-$0.45\,pp & .72 \\
CoNLL & 2{,}756 & .908 & 61.7\% & .302 & $-$0.85\,pp & 1.0 \\
SciERC & 529 & .644 & 17.8\% & .205 & $+$0.61\,pp & .193 \\
Few-NERD & 32{,}565 & .748 & 13.0\% & .220 & $\mathbf{+1.39}$\,pp & ${<}.001$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

Few-NERD (Table~\ref{tab:fewnerd}) confirms LP selection at low degeneracy ($+1.39$~pp, $p{<}0.001$; structural signals degrade: VC $-1.79$, SJ $-2.14$~pp), with LP 50$\times$ more seed-stable ($\sigma{=}0.04$ vs.\ SJ $2.02$~pp), generalizing to LLaMA (Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}). Training convergence modulates degeneracy (5-epoch: $31$\%, halving LP gains; Appendix~\ref{app:epoch_confound}).

\paragraph{Degeneracy alone does not predict LP direction.}
Degeneracy does not predict LP gain across 23 configurations ($\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$; post-hoc: 80\% power requires $n{\approx}85$; Figure~\ref{fig:degeneracy_scatter}), though it co-varies with LP within fixed settings ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}).

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_degeneracy_lp_scatter.pdf}
\caption{Instance degeneracy vs.\ LP selection gain across 23 configurations ($N{=}8$; $\rho{=}0.052$, $p{=}0.81$). Within Few-NERD per-entity-type ($\rho{=}{-}0.881$; Appendix~\ref{app:per_type_lp}), degeneracy predicts LP gain when alignment is held constant.}
\label{fig:degeneracy_scatter}
\end{figure}

Structural signals remain negative regardless of sample budget; $N$-scaling analyses are in Appendix~\ref{app:n_scaling}.

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Robustness}\label{sec:robustness_main}

Signal rankings are robust across temperatures, seeds ($W{=}1.0$), and $N$ (Table~\ref{tab:robustness}); NER calibration is reasonable (ECE 0.069--0.096; Appendix~\ref{app:calibration}).

\paragraph{Scale and controlled ablations.}
Qwen3-4B QE is comparable to 8B ($|\Delta\rho|{=}0.042$; Table~\ref{tab:scale_ablation_app}), yet selection remains below greedy.
LoRA rank${=}8$ lowers degeneracy to 7.2\% but LP stays below greedy, confirming both conditions are independently necessary; supervised classifiers, DeBERTa verifier, and hidden-state probes also fail (Appendix~\ref{app:supervised_combination}, \ref{app:hidden_probe}).

%% ============================================================
\subsection{Prescriptive Results}\label{sec:prescriptive}

\paragraph{Entity-level construction.}
We aggregate entities across samples, retaining those exceeding $\theta{=}2/N$, with LP-weighted or uniform scoring ($\theta$ stable in $[0.15, 0.35]$; Appendix~\ref{app:theta_sweep}). In zero-shot, LP-weighting is necessary (SciERC $+1.5$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:cross_scale}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Entity construction vs.\ greedy ($\Delta$ macro F1; $N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$, Qwen3-8B, multi-seed mean${\pm}\sigma$). $\theta{=}2/N$. $p$: paired bootstrap vs.\ greedy. $^\dagger$Survives Holm--Bonferroni within construction family ($k{=}10$; global $k{=}57$ in Appendix~\ref{app:holm}). $^\ddagger$LP-weighted not computed.}
\label{tab:entity_construction}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Wt. & Greedy & Constr. & $\Delta$ & $p$ \\
\midrule
SciERC & LP & $.646{\pm}.002$ & $\mathbf{.656{\pm}.003}$ & $+1.02{\pm}0.49$\,pp & .004 \\
       & Unif. & $.646{\pm}.002$ & $.653{\pm}.006$ & $+0.74{\pm}0.71$\,pp & .023 \\
\addlinespace
Few-NERD & LP & $.748$ & $\mathbf{.762{\pm}.005}$ & $+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp & ${<}.001^\dagger$ \\
         & Unif. & $.748$ & $.756{\pm}.006$ & $+0.74{\pm}0.58$\,pp & .003 \\
\addlinespace
CoNLL$^\ddagger$ & Unif. & .908 & .904 & $-0.39$\,pp & n.s. \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\end{table}

LP-weighted construction improves on Few-NERD ($+1.40{\pm}0.51$\,pp, $p{<}0.001$; Holm $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.006$, $k{=}10$) and SciERC ($+1.02{\pm}0.49$\,pp, $p{=}0.004$, $p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.20$), recovering 8--11\% of oracle headroom; CoNLL shows no gain ($-0.39$\,pp), consistent with high degeneracy.
Gains require both low degeneracy \textit{and} sufficient oracle headroom (Few-NERD: $D{=}13$\%, headroom${=}12.4$\,pp).\footnote{Holm--Bonferroni correction within families: selection $k{=}19$, construction $k{=}10$, signal $k{=}9$. Under global $k{=}57$, no claim survives ($p_{\text{adj}}{=}0.055$ for Few-NERD); see Appendix~\ref{app:holm}.}

\paragraph{Complementary strategies.}
Construction and LP selection are complementary, governed by within-instance LP alignment $\rho_{\text{adj}}$ (Few-NERD $0.516$ favors both; SciERC $0.167$ favors construction only).
QE signals also support selective prediction: at 80\% coverage, filtering yields $+5$--$8$\,pp F1 (AUPRC 0.70--0.92; Table~\ref{tab:cross_selection}).

\phantomsection\label{sec:selection_results}

\paragraph{Bootstrap significance at $N{=}32$.}
Table~\ref{tab:bootstrap_n32} reports bootstrap confidence intervals and $p$-values for selection at $N{=}32$ ($B{=}10{,}000$, $n{=}529$).
No signal achieves significant improvement over greedy at $\alpha{=}0.05$ (LP: $p{=}0.091$; FK: $p{=}0.101$), while VC is significantly \textit{worse} ($-4.36$~pp, $p{=}1.0$).
However, all signals except VC significantly outperform random selection ($p{<}0.001$, permutation test); the signals carry meaningful quality information yet fail to translate it into reliable selection gains.
The oracle gap ($+20.0$~pp, $p{<}0.001$) confirms substantial unrealized headroom.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Bootstrap significance test at $N{=}32$ (SciERC NER, Qwen3-8B, $T{=}1.0$, $B{=}10{,}000$, $n{=}529$ gold-non-empty instances; 22 instances with no gold entities are excluded as they contribute zero selection differential). $\Delta$: selection gain vs.\ greedy. CI: 95\% bootstrap percentile interval. $p_{\text{greedy}}$: one-sided $p$-value vs.\ greedy; $p_{\text{rand}}$: permutation test vs.\ random selection.}
\label{tab:bootstrap_n32}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
Signal & $\Delta$ (pp) & 95\% CI & $p_{\text{greedy}}$ & $p_{\text{rand}}$ \\
\midrule
LP & $+$0.95 & [$-$0.41, $+$2.34] & 0.091 & ${<}$0.001 \\
FK & $+$0.66 & [$-$0.36, $+$1.67] & 0.101 & ${<}$0.001 \\
SJ & $+$0.36 & [$-$0.66, $+$1.38] & 0.244 & ${<}$0.001 \\
EM & $+$0.22 & [$-$0.99, $+$1.44] & 0.355 & ${<}$0.001 \\
VC & $-$4.36 & [$-$6.26, $-$2.37] & 1.000 & 0.741 \\
\midrule
Oracle & $+$20.00 & [$+$17.98, $+$22.10] & ${<}$0.001 & --- \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Oracle ceiling scaling law.}
Oracle F1 follows $F_1(N) = a - b \cdot N^{-c}$, with $R^2 > 0.999$ across all model--dataset fits (Figure~\ref{fig:oracle_scaling}).
The exponent $c$ varies with dataset complexity: FewNERD ($c{\approx}0.61$) scales comparably to reasoning-task estimates ($c{\approx}0.5$--$0.7$; \citealp{brown2024monkeys}), while SciERC converges more slowly ($c{\approx}0.35$), consistent with complex IE producing less marginal diversity per additional sample.
CoNLL's narrow headroom ($b{=}0.04$--$0.10$) makes $c$ unreliable (inter-model variance $2.5{\times}$); we report only $a$ and $b$ for CoNLL.
A single stochastic sample ($N{=}1$, $T{=}1.0$) underperforms greedy by 1--3\,pp because sampling noise outweighs diversity; $N{\geq}4$ is needed to match greedy.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_oracle_scaling.pdf}
\caption{Oracle $F_1$ as a function of sample count $N$, fitted with $F_1(N) = a - b \cdot N^{-c}$. All fits $R^2 > 0.999$. Horizontal dashed lines: greedy ($T{=}0$) F1. The ceiling grows monotonically with $N$, but the correlation-selection gap (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}) limits how much is actually recoverable.}
\label{fig:oracle_scaling}
\end{figure}

\section{Cross-Scale and Cross-Family Comparison}\label{app:cross_scale}

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-scale and cross-family comparison ($N{=}8$, $T{=}1.0$). Hdr.\ = oracle $-$ greedy (pp). Degen.: instance degeneracy (\%). $^\star$Qwen2.5; unmarked: Qwen3. $^\dagger$Not computed. $^\ddagger$200-instance subset; 10.1\% parse failures excluded.}
\label{tab:scale_comparison}
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccccccc@{}}
\toprule
Dataset & Regime & Greedy & Hdr. & Degen. & VC & SJ & FK & LP \\
\midrule
SciERC & 8B FT  & .644 & 13.0 & 17.8 & .379 & .360 & .267 & .205 \\
       & 72B ZS$^\star$ & .399 & 15.0 & 21.0 & .277 & .217 & .227 & .081 \\
\midrule
Few-NERD & 7B FT$^\star$ & .784 & 9.2 & 31.7 & .404 & .388 & .315 & .346 \\
         & 8B FT & .748 & 12.4 & 13.0 & .386 & .300 & .313 & .220 \\
         & 14B FT & .798 & 8.1 & ---$^\dagger$ & ---$^\dagger$ & ---$^\dagger$ & ---$^\dagger$ & ---$^\dagger$ \\
         & 72B ZS$^\star$ & .628 & ---$^\dagger$ & 46.2 & .416 & .345 & ---$^\dagger$ & .119 \\

VC & 0.0187 & 0.0260 & 0.0244 & 0.0158 \\
EM & 0.0076 & 0.0031 & 0.0157 & 0.0049 \\
LP & 0.0125 & 0.0017 & 0.0218 & 0.0152 \\
\midrule
Mean & 0.0145 & 0.0098 & 0.0151 & 0.0114 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Signal rankings are consistent across our 3 seeds (Kendall's $W{=}1.000$ for NER at $N{=}8$, both models), dropping to $W{=}0.956$ at $N{=}16$ where SJ and VC converge (Table~\ref{tab:ranking-stability}); however, with only $n{=}3$ seeds statistical power is insufficient to confirm stability, and additional seeds may reveal ranking changes.
Doubling $N$ reduces cross-seed $\sigma$ by 32\% on NER and 24\% on RE (Table~\ref{tab:sigma-comparison}), with the largest reductions for SJ ($-43\%$ NER) and EM ($-59\%$ NER); additional samples stabilize structural signals more than LP.

\paragraph{$N$-scaling: selection headroom vs.\ selection gain.}
Table~\ref{tab:n_scaling_selection} traces the effect of sample count on degeneracy, oracle headroom, and per-signal selection gain.
Oracle headroom grows roughly linearly with $N$ ($+4.4$~pp at $N{=}2$ to $+20.0$~pp at $N{=}32$).
LP selection improves monotonically with $N$ ($+0.39$~pp at $N{=}8$ to $+0.95$~pp at $N{=}32$), but even at $N{=}32$, where degeneracy falls to 4\% and oracle headroom reaches ${\sim}20$~pp, the best signal captures less than 5\% of the available improvement.
Degeneracy drops monotonically (43\% at $N{=}2$ to 4\% at $N{=}32$), yet reduced degeneracy does not unlock selection, consistent with the LoRA-rank ablation finding (\S\ref{sec:robustness_main}).
VC selection degrades with increasing $N$ ($-2.66$~pp at $N{=}2$ to $-4.35$~pp at $N{=}32$), consistent with mode-seeking bias in voting-based selection as the candidate pool grows.

